From a35aec9605aa6b9c7d35034af913340a101dbef6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Brocklehurst <matt@mattbrocklehurst.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 02:29:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: host: bcm2835: guard against host->data already being
 NULL in bcm2835_finish_data()

Real crash, confirmed live via CI serial console on QEMU (raspi2b,
after the dma-ranges fix that got boot far enough to actually complete
a DMA transfer for the first time):

  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 00000014
  PC is at bcm2835_finish_data+0x28/0x60
  ...
  bcm2835_finish_data from bcm2835_dma_complete_work+0xf8/0x124

Root cause: two independent completion signals can race for the same
MMC transfer. bcm2835_finish_data() itself clears host->data = NULL
(via bcm2835_transfer_complete()) when a transfer completes through
the interrupt/status-polling path. bcm2835_dma_complete_work() -- a
workqueue callback fired by the DMA engine's own completion
notification -- calls bcm2835_finish_data() unconditionally, with no
check for whether the same transfer already finished (and cleared
host->data) via that other path first. When it loses the race,
host->data is NULL and the very next line (data->error) is a NULL
deref.

This was very likely always a latent bug rather than something the
dma-ranges devicetree fix introduced: before that fix, no DMA
transfer on this driver ever completed successfully at all (the very
first dma_map_phys() call always failed), so this code path was never
reached. Fixing the mapping failure exposed a second, previously
unreachable bug behind it -- not something we caused, something we
uncovered.

Not yet confirmed whether this is purely a QEMU-timing artifact (DMA
completion racing the status-poll path in a way real hardware's
timing may rarely hit) or a genuine upstream race also latent on real
hardware. Either way, unconditionally dereferencing a pointer that can
legitimately be NULL here is wrong regardless of environment -- this
guard is correct either way, and unblocks CI boot testing tonight
without requiring a full explanation of the race's real-hardware
likelihood first.

Signed-off-by: Matt Brocklehurst <matt@mattbrocklehurst.co.uk>
---
 drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835.c b/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835.c
index 2278caee3650..73ed0c57d98b 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835.c
@@ -746,6 +746,20 @@ static void bcm2835_finish_data(struct bcm2835_host *host)
 	struct mmc_data *data;
 
 	data = host->data;
+	/*
+	 * host->data (and the transfer it points to) can already have been
+	 * completed and cleared by the time this runs: bcm2835_dma_complete_work()
+	 * calls this unconditionally from a workqueue, but the same transfer
+	 * may already have finished via the interrupt/status-polling path
+	 * (bcm2835_finish_data() -> bcm2835_transfer_complete(), which sets
+	 * host->data = NULL) before the deferred DMA-completion callback runs.
+	 * Without this guard that's a NULL pointer dereference on data->error
+	 * a few lines down. Confirmed live: "Unable to handle kernel NULL
+	 * pointer dereference at virtual address 00000014", PC at
+	 * bcm2835_finish_data+0x28/0x60, called from bcm2835_dma_complete_work.
+	 */
+	if (!data)
+		return;
 
 	host->hcfg &= ~(SDHCFG_DATA_IRPT_EN | SDHCFG_BLOCK_IRPT_EN);
 	writel(host->hcfg, host->ioaddr + SDHCFG);
-- 
